WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.950
I've been covering finance for nearly a decade,
and I've written about some wild stuff, but

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:09.380
there's no issue I've covered that causes
more stress in people's lives than student loans.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.820
Some one in six American adults has a student
loan.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:19.179
A quarter of them are having trouble paying
it back, and it's no wonder.

00:00:19.179 --> 00:00:23.319
It's a horribly and needlessly complicated
system.

00:00:23.319 --> 00:00:25.650
It's not easy to make sense of your finances.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.519
I'm Shahien Nasiripour, and I want to meet
people with an obsession to beat the system

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:40.120
and never leave money on the table.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.379
Consider this.

00:00:41.379 --> 00:00:44.480
The Feds offer borrowers about six weeks to
get their loans forgiven.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.769
One of the most prominent ways, taking a public
service job after college has a 99% rejection

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:51.999
rate.

00:00:51.999 --> 00:00:56.030
Licensed clinical social worker, Michael Mitchell,
is part of the one percent.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.929
He's one of the first to make it through the
Federal Public Service Loan Forgiveness program,

00:00:59.929 --> 00:01:01.699
which started in 2007.

00:01:01.699 --> 00:01:05.040
I visited his office in Manhattan to find
out how.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:10.420
By the time I got through this whole process
and got the forgiveness, it was actually $170,000

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.420
at that point.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:12.420
Wow.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.510
I just remember seeing my net worth one day
going from here to here.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:18.510
Did you call anyone?

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.510
Did you tell anyone?

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:20.930
I spoke with my best friend.

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.570
We popped a bottle of champagne.

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:23.570
Did you really?

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:24.570
Yeah.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.920
In 2007, Congress created Public Service Loan
Forgiveness.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:32.100
The pitch for borrowers was you spend 10 years
as a nurse, police officer ... Basically,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.010
work for a government agency or a non-profit,
and after 12 on time payments a year for a

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.660
decade, whatever's left of your debt is wiped
away.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.430
It's a huge benefit available to about a quarter
of all Americans with a job.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.010
I downloaded the law and I read it.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:55.990
I had been a proofreader before, so I'm just
somebody who's very detailed oriented.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:01.450
At this point, it was probably $130,000, $140,000
that I owed because it just kept growing,

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:06.270
even though I was already paying loan payments
at that point.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.380
Your balance was shooting up?

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.680
My balance was continuing to creep up.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.390
Right.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:22.850
when filing these forms that they did work
in eligible employment, and then maybe four

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.860
years through the process-
It didn't count.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:25.860
Yeah.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:26.860
It didn't count.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:27.860
They backtracked on that.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:32.020
It sounds simple when you just toss it off
of like, “Oh yeah, you work 10 years in

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.190
not for profit, and your loans are forgiven.”

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.890
But, there's a lot of nuance to it.

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:42.100
How often did you call your loan servicer
to talk about this?

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.760
By the end, I was calling them probably every
other day.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:47.760
Wow.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.460
But, public service isn't the only way to
get your student debt forgiven.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.990
You can die, for example.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:54.990
Sorry.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.780
It's a bit grim, but it is true.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:01.670
Or, if you're totally disabled or so destitute
that you'll never be able to afford your payments,

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:03.740
the Feds will forgive your loans then, too.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.480
The other big way is to enroll in plans that
tie your monthly payments to your income,

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:10.209
like Michael did while he was in the public
service plan.

00:03:10.209 --> 00:03:14.110
Here's Bloomberg reporter, Janet Lorin, on
how borrowers can pay less on their loans.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:16.140
Caution, there's a warning coming.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:22.350
There are programs called Income-Based Repayment,
and students could qualify based on what they're

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.350
making.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.560
You also have to be mindful that the interest
is going to be accruing.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:33.790
There's this scary term called negative amortization,
and

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:38.180
that means even though you may not be making
a payment, or you're making a small payment,

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.880
the total balance of your loan is continuing
to grow.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:42.880
Thanks, Janet.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.780
Let's look at the scary math Janet just laid
out.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:53.349
Imagine you make 45 grand a year, and you
owe 50 grand at six percent interest.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:58.209
Now, you could try to pay it off over 10 years
by making monthly payments of about $550.00,

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:03.930
or you could opt into the income based plan,
and pay as little as $220.00 a month, but

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:04.990
consider this.

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:10.000
Under the first option, your total payments,
including interest, would come out to about

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.430
66 grand.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.359
Under the second, because of all that interest
and because you'd be paying over about twice

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:20.529
the amount of time, you pay more than 92 grand.

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:24.499
Student loans can have really steep interest
rates, and if you can afford it, you'd be

00:04:24.499 --> 00:04:29.419
better off paying a bit more than required
because it'd be a smart way to avoid paying

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:31.210
unnecessary interest.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:36.029
Remember, generally whatever interest you
don't pay just gets added to your total balance.

00:04:36.029 --> 00:04:39.030
A lot of people are paying interest on interest.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:42.300
It's stressful, but meeting stress head on
can feel good.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.220
Give it the old college try.

